<div style="background-color: #e0f2f7; border: 2px solid #a7d9ed; padding: 15px; text-align: center; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Where Is Your Book? – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; color: #1a5276;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> Kinder (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Where Is It?</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Where Is Your Book?</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From <span style="text-decoration: underline;">___</span> to <span style="text-decoration: underline;">___</span></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Follow very simple classroom instructions with visual cues.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can follow simple instructions with visual cues (e.g., 'Go to the door.' or 'Show me the book.'). Can follow along during group story time, with gestures or with single-word responses (e.g., 'Where's the dog?' and the student responds, 'There.'). Can respond to simple questions by pointing or with single-word responses (e.g., 'Where is the pen?' and the student responds, 'There' or 'Where is the notebook? Is it on the desk or under the desk?' and the student responds, 'On').</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 34%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts with a "Where Is It?" song and actions. Students listen and follow simple commands using TPR. For example, "Where is your head? (point to head). Where are your hands? (shake hands). Where is the book? (point to a book on a desk)." The teacher uses simple objects like a book and a desk in the classroom. This activates prior knowledge of body parts and introduces the idea of location.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a book and a desk. The teacher places the book <b>on</b> the desk and says, "The book is <b>on</b> the desk." The teacher places the book <b>under</b> the desk and says, "The book is <b>under</b> the desk." The teacher models the pronunciation of /w/ in 'where' (e.g., 'Where is it?') and /b/ in 'book' (e.g., 'book,' 'bag') clearly and slowly, encouraging choral repetition.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher places a book on the desk) "Is the book <b>on</b> the desk or <b>under</b> the desk?" (Teacher points to 'on' and 'under' visuals/actions). Students respond by pointing or with "On!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher places a pencil in a box) "Show me '<b>in</b>'!" Students point to the pencil inside the box.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Date: ___</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Time: 7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Where Is It?"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Large, colorful picture flashcards (or a projected image) showing a classroom scene with various objects (book, pencil, bag, desk, chair, box, poster) in different locations (in, on, under, next to, behind). The material is a short, descriptive narrative/dialogue format (teacher-led) about finding classroom items.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Using location words: Respond with basic words to location-based questions, using prepositions (e.g., 'The book is on the desk.'). Following location commands: Move to designated areas in response to instructions (e.g., 'Go to the window.').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Questions:</b> The teacher shows the flashcards and narrates simple sentences like, "Look! Here is a classroom. The book is <b>on</b> the desk. The pencil is <b>in</b> the box. The bag is <b>under</b> the chair. The poster is <b>next to</b> the door." (Teacher points to each item and location as described). Teacher asks, "What do you see?" Students point to objects they recognize.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (Pointing):</b> The teacher says a sentence about the picture, sometimes true, sometimes false, e.g., "The book is under the chair." (pointing to a book on the desk). Students listen and show "thumbs up" for true or "thumbs down" for false.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching (Pointing):</b> The teacher asks, "Where is the pencil?" and students listen and point to the pencil in the picture. The teacher asks, "Show me the bag <b>under</b> the chair." Students listen and point to the correct location.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Date: ___</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Time: 10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> Prepositions of place (in, on, under, next to, behind). WH-Questions for Location ('Where is the book? It is on the desk.'). Plural nouns in simple responses (e.g., 'two books').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> Nouns: chair, desk, table, book, bag, box, pencil, poster. Verbs: point, hold, pick, put, show, go. Adjectives: large, small. Colors: yellow, red, blue.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> /w/ in 'where', /b/ in 'book'. Phonemic awareness: Recognizing letter sounds (K, L, M, N, O).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pragmatic:</b> Using location words. Following location commands.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Sociolinguistic:</b> Giving information about locations of objects.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice: "Listen and Do" (TPR):</b> The teacher uses real classroom objects (a book, a pencil, a box, a bag, a chair, a desk) and gives simple commands. Students listen carefully and perform the action. Examples: "<b>Put</b> the <b>book on</b> the <b>desk</b>!" "<b>Pick</b> up the <b>pencil</b>." "<b>Go to</b> the <b>chair</b>." "<b>Show me</b> the <b>bag under</b> the <b>table</b>." The teacher models pronunciation of target sounds (/w/, /b/) throughout.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice: "Where is it? Listen and Point!":</b> The teacher places several objects around the classroom or on a large picture mat. The teacher asks, "<b>Where is</b> the <b>book</b>?" and points to a student. The student listens and points to the book, then the teacher might prompt, "Is it <b>on</b> or <b>under</b>?" for a single-word response. The teacher also uses plural nouns in questions: "<b>Where are</b> the <b>pencils</b>?" (Teacher holds two pencils) "Put the <b>two books on</b> the <b>desk</b>." Students respond by pointing or with single words (e.g., "On," "Under," "There").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction/Peer Check (Listen and Match):</b> Students work in pairs with a small collection of object cards (book, pencil, bag) and a mat featuring simple drawings of a desk, chair, and box. Student A gives a simple instruction like, "<b>Book on</b> the <b>desk</b>!" Student B listens and places the book card on the desk drawing. They check each other's actions (peer check through observation) and then swap roles. Teacher circulates, providing support and correcting listening comprehension.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Date: ___</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Time: 15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Where Is It?"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: "Listen and Draw/Arrange My Classroom"</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students receive a worksheet with a blank outline of a classroom or a blank space, and small cut-outs of classroom objects (book, pencil, bag, desk, chair, box). The teacher gives a series of instructions. Students listen and either draw the objects in the specified locations or arrange the cut-outs on their worksheet according to the instructions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher starts with simple instructions: "<b>Draw</b> a <b>desk</b>." "<b>Draw</b> a <b>chair next to</b> the <b>desk</b>." "<b>Put</b> the <b>book on</b> the <b>desk</b>." "<b>Put</b> the <b>pencil in</b> the <b>box</b>." "<b>Go to</b> your <b>table</b>."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher incorporates colors and adjectives: "<b>Put</b> the <b>blue book under</b> the <b>large chair</b>." "<b>Draw</b> a <b>small red bag behind</b> the <b>desk</b>." The teacher also practices polite requests: "<b>Show me</b> the <b>blue crayon, please</b>." (Teacher asks this, and students listen and point to their blue crayon).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The focus is on students' ability to listen to increasingly complex instructions and accurately represent the information visually. The teacher walks around, observes, and provides individual support as needed.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Date: ___</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Time: 8 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist during "Listen and Follow" activity.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task: "Listen and Follow – Classroom Edition"</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher gives 3-4 multi-step instructions using classroom objects and prepositions. Students demonstrate comprehension by performing the actions. Examples: "<b>Pick up</b> the <b>yellow book</b> and <b>put</b> it <b>under</b> your <b>chair</b>." "<b>Go to</b> the <b>table</b> and <b>show me</b> the <b>blue pencil in</b> the <b>box</b>." "<b>Hold</b> your <b>bag behind</b> your <b>desk</b>."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can follow 1-step instructions with visual cues (e.g., 'Book on the desk').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can follow 2-step instructions with visual cues (e.g., 'Pick up the pencil, then put it in the box').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Responds to 'Where is...?' questions by pointing or with single-word responses (e.g., 'On,' 'Under,' 'There').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Evidence of recognizing /w/ and /b/ sounds (through participation in activities).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher asks while showing visuals) "Did I listen carefully today?" Students show thumbs up (yes) or thumbs down (no).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher shows a picture of a book on a desk) "Can you find the book <b>on</b> the desk?" Students point to the book.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Date: ___</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Time: 6 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was your favorite listening game today?" (Students point to an activity or make a sound indicating their choice).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new words did you learn about 'where' things are?" (Teacher points to 'in,' 'on,' 'under' pictures and students respond by pointing or saying the word if able).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this listening lesson for Kinder? (e.g., TPR was highly effective for 'in,' 'on,' 'under'; visual cues enhanced comprehension; the "Listen and Point" game kept students engaged.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What challenges did students face? What adjustments/scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? (Some students struggled with 'behind' and 'next to' without very clear visual distinctions. I need to use more exaggerated actions or distinct realia for these prepositions. Instructions needed to be repeated slowly. More repetition of plural nouns in context would be beneficial. I will ensure simpler sentences in the next lesson's reading text).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Connection to next lesson: Lesson 2 will focus on Reading Comprehension. Students will encounter the same vocabulary (classroom objects) and prepositions (in, on, under, next to, behind) in a simple, illustrated story or short text, building on their listening comprehension from today.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Date: ___</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Time: 4 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>